<Styles xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:controls="using:Lightbox.App.Controls">
  <!--
    The density scale from .claude/quality/DESIGN.md, in one place.

    Chrome is measured against the canvas it takes from: a drawing app spends
    every pixel of panel on something that is not the work. These styles are
    what stop each view inventing its own height — a control that sets its own
    size is how a panel drifts away from the ones beside it.

    Set here, not per view. If a control needs to break the scale it says why
    in a comment at the site.
  -->

  <!-- Numeric fields: no spinner arrows anywhere.

       The up/down buttons cost ~20px of width and a couple of height, and no
       one uses them — a value is typed, or dragged on the slider beside it.
       Removing them keeps the parsing, clamping and formatting NumericUpDown
       exists for, and loses only the part that was in the way. -->
  <Style Selector="NumericUpDown">
    <Setter Property="ShowButtonSpinner" Value="False" />
    <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="22" />
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="22" />
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="11" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <!-- Every numeric field scrubs: press and drag sideways. Set here so it
         is true of all of them rather than the ones somebody remembered. -->
    <Setter Property="controls:ValueDrag.Enabled" Value="True" />
  </Style>
  <!--
    One width for every value field in the tool options bar, and one track
    length for every slider.

    Sizing each pair to taste gave a row whose labels, tracks and boxes started
    and ended at different places every time the tool changed — the boxes were
    68, 72 and 64 wide for three numbers of the same shape. A bar you read
    left to right has to be a column of columns, and that is only true if the
    parts are the same size.
  -->
  <Style Selector="NumericUpDown.value">
    <Setter Property="Width" Value="64" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Right" />
  </Style>

  <Style Selector="Slider.param">
    <Setter Property="Width" Value="96" />
  </Style>

  <!--
    The inner box a NumericUpDown wraps. Its `PART_BorderElement` paints the
    ground on top of the NumericUpDown's own rounded border, so without a
    matching radius here the corners come out square while the ComboBox beside
    it is round — which is what shipped, and what "every boxed element has
    rounded corners except those text boxes" was reporting.
  -->
  <Style Selector="NumericUpDown /template/ TextBox">
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="4,0" />
    <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="20" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource FieldCornerRadius}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="NumericUpDown /template/ TextBox /template/ Border#PART_BorderElement">
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource FieldCornerRadius}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="NumericUpDown">
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource FieldCornerRadius}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="TextBox">
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource FieldCornerRadius}" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="ComboBox">
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource FieldCornerRadius}" />
  </Style>

  <Style Selector="TextBox">
    <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="24" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="6,2" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
  </Style>

  <Style Selector="ComboBox">
    <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="24" />
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="24" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="6,0" />
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="11" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
  </Style>

  <!-- The default for every button: one height, one padding, one font size.
       Deliberately NO MinWidth here — timeline cels and the stacked reorder
       arrows are buttons too, and a global minimum width would wreck both. -->
  <Style Selector="Button">
    <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="24" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="8,0" />
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="11" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
  </Style>

  <!-- Text buttons that sit in a row together. MinWidth is what makes
       "Import…" and "Export…" line up instead of each hugging its own label,
       which is the single most common way a bar here looked unfinished. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.text">
    <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="70" />
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="24" />
  </Style>

  <!--
    Icon buttons: square, tight, and all the same. Opt in with Classes="icon".

    **26, and it is the glyph that decides it rather than the scale.** The row
    height an artist sees is this number, so 24 would be the tidier answer and
    it does not survive contact with the content: the icons are emoji — 👁 🔒 ＋
    ✕ — and a 16px emoji with an ascender does not fit a 24px tile once the
    border and the line box are paid for. It clips rather than crowds, which is
    the version nobody notices until it is shipped. The overlay bars found this
    first (see the tile below) and this is the same argument in the second place
    it applies.

    So this is the one number on the scale that is waiting on something else:
    when the icon set lands and these stop being emoji, 26 can go to 20 and the
    rows with it. `docs/DESIGN-ui-system.md` records that dependency, because
    the design reference's 21px rows are otherwise unreachable and the next
    person to measure the mockup will re-derive the blocker.

    **Both variants declare the same square.** They did not: Button.icon pinned
    Width/MaxWidth/Height and ToggleButton.icon set only MinWidth and Height, so
    a toggle beside a button in the same row could come out wider. A row of
    tiles with different proportions stops reading as a row, which is the defect
    the overlay bar's fixed square already exists to prevent.
  -->
  <Style Selector="Button.icon">
    <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="26" />
    <Setter Property="MaxWidth" Value="26" />
    <Setter Property="Width" Value="26" />
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="26" />
    <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="26" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="ClipToBounds" Value="False" />
  </Style>

  <Style Selector="ToggleButton">
    <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="24" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="8,0" />
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="11" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="ToggleButton.icon">
    <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="26" />
    <Setter Property="MaxWidth" Value="26" />
    <Setter Property="Width" Value="26" />
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="26" />
    <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="26" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="ClipToBounds" Value="False" />
  </Style>

  <!--
    The canvas overlay bars are a grid of icon tiles, so every tile is the
    same square.

    They were sizing to their glyph, and glyphs are not the same width: ◉ and
    ▶ measured 25, an emoji like 🎥 or 🔒 measured more, and the bar's own ▾
    and ✕ measured 24. Nothing was wrong with any one of them, which is how a
    ragged column of buttons survives every review that looks at one control
    at a time. A fixed square is the only thing that makes a tile grid read as
    a grid.
  -->
  <!--
    26 rather than 24, and it is the same argument one size up. At 24 the taller
    glyphs were *clipped* rather than ragged — a 16px emoji with an ascender does
    not fit a 24px tile once the border and the line box are paid for — so the
    fixed square was doing its job and the square was too small. Raised here, in
    the one place that decides it, so every tile moves together and the grid
    still reads as a grid.
  -->
  <!--
    BELOW the plain Button and ToggleButton rules, and it has to be.

    **Avalonia resolves competing styles by order, not by specificity** — the
    last matching setter wins — and this block used to sit above them. So the
    generic `Padding="8,0"` overrode the `Padding="0"` here, every overlay tile
    spent 16 of its 26 px on padding, and the wide emoji were cut off. The
    selector looks more specific and counts for nothing; only its position does.
    That is why the raise from 24 to 26 did not fix the clipping it was aimed at:
    the tile grew and the content box did not.

    Anything targeted enough to beat a base rule belongs after it. The `.icon`
    and `.text` classes above are already arranged that way, which is what made
    this one the odd rule out rather than the pattern.
  -->
  <Style Selector="controls|CanvasOverlayBar Button, controls|CanvasOverlayBar ToggleButton">
    <Setter Property="Width" Value="26" />
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="26" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="ClipToBounds" Value="False" />
  </Style>

  <Style Selector="CheckBox">
    <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="22" />
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="11" />
  </Style>

  <!--
    Sliders keep a generous TRACK — they are dragged mid-stroke, often without
    looking, and a thin track is a missed grab — but not a generous thumb. The
    default Fluent thumb is sized for touch, and at this density it is the
    single loudest thing in a tool bar full of quiet ones.
  -->
  <Style Selector="Slider">
    <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="22" />
    <!--
      Centre the slider against the label beside it.

      Fluent's slider template reserves room above and below the track for a
      tick strip nothing here uses, so the track sat high in the space the
      control was given and read as misaligned next to a centred label. It was.
    -->
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0" />
    <!--
      A real height, not just a minimum, and this is the one that matters.

      Fluent's slider asks for 44px whatever you set MinHeight to — the tick
      strip above and below the track is measured even when it is empty. The
      tool options bar is 30px, so the group holding a slider overflowed it,
      and an overflowing child cannot be centred: Avalonia pins it to the top
      and lets it hang out of the bottom. Its label then sat 7px lower than
      every group without a slider in it, which is exactly the drift that
      shows up in a screenshot and never in a width check.

      Setting the height makes every group in the bar ask for the same 24, so
      they all centre on the same line. The track and the 14px thumb both fit
      inside it with room to spare.
    -->
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="24" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Slider:horizontal /template/ Grid#SliderContainer">
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Slider:horizontal /template/ Track">
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Slider:horizontal /template/ Thumb">
    <Setter Property="Width" Value="14" />
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="14" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="Slider:vertical /template/ Thumb">
    <Setter Property="Width" Value="14" />
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="14" />
  </Style>

  <!--
    A ComboBox declares Height, which in Avalonia stretches the control but
    leaves its own content wherever the template put it — top, in this theme.
    Beside a row of centred buttons that reads as a misalignment, and it is.
  -->
  <Style Selector="ComboBox">
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="ComboBox /template/ ContentControl#ContentPresenter">
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="ComboBox /template/ ContentPresenter">
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
  </Style>

  <!--
    The menu bar's own row.

    Fluent gives a top-level menu item generous padding, which is right for a
    menu that drops down over a document and wrong for the four words that sit
    permanently above the canvas. `File Edit View Help` is the least
    information-dense strip in the application and it was the tallest.

    Only the top level, and the items *inside* a dropdown are left alone — those
    genuinely want the room, because a menu is read while pointing at it and a
    mis-click there picks the wrong command.

    **Opted into with a class rather than selected structurally**, and that is
    not a style preference. `Menu > MenuItem` looks like the obvious selector
    and matches nothing: Avalonia's child combinator walks the *visual* tree,
    and a menu's items are inside an ItemsPresenter, so the Menu is their
    grandparent. It fails silently — no error, no warning, the bar simply stays
    the height it was. Found by measuring the strip and seeing one number
    refuse to move.

    **`Height` rather than `Padding`, which is the second thing that failed
    here.** With the class applying and `Padding="10,3"` set, the item still
    measured 32: the theme binds our padding to the header presenter's *margin*
    and then centres that presenter in an item it has already sized, so padding
    moves the text within the row without changing the row. Probing the tree is
    what showed it — the presenter sat at y=8 in a 32-tall item with a 3px top
    margin, which is a number that cannot be explained by the padding it came
    from. One of the few places here where naming a height is the honest lever
    rather than the lazy one.
  -->
  <!--
    The bar and its items both, because they are sized independently: with the
    items at 24 the Menu still reserved 32 and centred them in it, so the strip
    kept its height and the words simply floated. Two numbers that have to
    agree, which is why they are adjacent here rather than one being left to
    follow the other.
  -->
  <Style Selector="Menu">
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0" />
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="24" />
  </Style>
  <Style Selector="MenuItem.top">
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="24" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="10,0" />
  </Style>

  <!--
    Tool buttons are hit while drawing; they keep the larger target.

    **34, which is what shipped rather than the 30 this used to claim.** The
    tool palette declared its own size in `MainWindow.axaml` and that override
    won, so the 30 here was never applied to a single control — a scale entry
    that describes nothing. Merged the other way round on purpose: the palette
    is the clearest case of "generous where a mistake costs a stroke", so the
    number an artist has been hitting is the one that is right, and it is the
    scale that had drifted away from it.

    The `labels` class on the toolbar (set from code-behind at a width
    threshold) reveals the names beside the icons; only the alignment changes
    there, never the height, so the column keeps one rhythm at both widths.
  -->
  <Style Selector="ToggleButton.tool">
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="34" />
    <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="34" />
    <Setter Property="Margin" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="4,0" />
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
  </Style>

  <!--
    The tool options bar is one fixed row, and this is the number every group in
    it is measured against by `NothingInTheBarAsksForMoreHeightThanTheBarHas`.
    It was declared inline on the control in `MainWindow.axaml`, which made it
    the one height in the application that no reader of the scale could find.
  -->
  <Style Selector="controls|OverflowBar">
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="30" />
  </Style>
</Styles>
